Bash(git commit -m "$(cat <<'EOF'
ci(#65): run test suite on PRs, independent of markdown paths filter

Today the workflow only triggers on push to main, and the only job that
calls `pnpm test` (validate-okf) is itself gated behind a markdown/mdx
paths filter — so a TypeScript- or shell-only change gets zero test
signal, whether on a PR or after merge.

Split `pnpm test` into its own `test` job with no `needs`/`if`, so it
always runs regardless of file type. Add `pull_request` as a workflow
trigger so PRs get CI feedback before merge, not just pushes to main.
`validate-okf` keeps its existing markdown-paths-filter gate unchanged.

Adding `pull_request` as a trigger has one side effect that needed
handling: the pre-existing `deploy` job had no `if` guard because `push`
to main was the only trigger it could ever see. With `pull_request` added,
`deploy` would otherwise also attempt to run on every PR — using AWS
deploy secrets against unreviewed PR code. Guarded it with
`if: github.event_name == 'push'` to preserve push-to-main-only semantics.

Verification: this sandbox has no `gh`/docker/act/network access, so
"verified against a real pull request" per the issue's acceptance
criteria could not be done literally. Verified instead by: running
`pnpm test` and `tsc --noEmit`/`eslint .` locally (clean); parsing the
resulting YAML with js-yaml to confirm job/if/needs structure; and a
manual trace of GitHub Actions trigger semantics for push-to-main,
PR-open, and PR-merge, confirming no double-triggering and no gap in
coverage for TS-only, shell-only, and markdown-only changes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
EOF
)"
git log --oneline -3)
Committed cleanly. The work is done: `.github/workflows/main.yml` now runs `pnpm test` in a standalone `test` job on every push and pull request (no longer gated behind the markdown paths filter), `validate-okf` keeps its unchanged markdown-gated behavior, and `deploy` is guarded to push-events-only so it doesn't fire on PRs now that `pull_request` is a trigger.

