## Review summary

**Standards axis** — no documented-standard violations (no CI/YAML style doc exists in this repo). Judgement-call nits only:
- `deploy` has no `needs: test`, so a failing test doesn't block deploy on `main` pushes — but this gap **pre-exists** the diff (confirmed via `git diff`: `deploy` had no `needs` before either), and gating deploy on test results isn't part of #65's acceptance criteria. Not a blocker for this issue.
- Minor `pull_request` (no branch filter) vs `push` (`branches: [main]`) trigger asymmetry — intentional, not a defect.
- Repeated checkout/pnpm-setup/install boilerplate is idiomatic for GitHub Actions (fresh VM per job), not a real smell.

**Spec axis** — **no findings**. All in-scope acceptance criteria are satisfied: `test` job runs unconditionally on `pull_request` and `push` with no paths-filter gating (covers TS-only and shell-only PRs identically, since the job doesn't discriminate by file type), `check-changes`/`validate-okf` are untouched and now also fire on PRs, and `deploy` is correctly guarded with `if: github.event_name == 'push'` to avoid firing on PRs. No scope creep.

Combined with the re-run verification (14/14 tests pass, `tsc --noEmit` exit 0, source-scoped `eslint` clean — the `eslint .` failures are entirely from a gitignored `.sandcastle/container-cache` Next.js build artifact left over in this container, not from the committed diff), the committed work (`196ee84`) holds up. No defects surfaced that warrant a change, so per the repair-context instructions I'm making no further commits.

